---
title: aggregate
---



`aggregate`

Moving data between spatial scales.

- ``aggregate`` goes *up* a nested hierarchy (e.g. municipality -> province
  -> country) using the published crosswalks and per-variable rules.
- ``overlay`` moves data between scales that do not nest (grids, custom zones,
  historical boundaries) by area-weighted interpolation.

## Functions

| Name | Description |
| --- | --- |
| [aggregate](#geo4bolivia.aggregate.aggregate) | Aggregate a panel up to a coarser, nested scale (or to several at once). |
| [overlay](#geo4bolivia.aggregate.overlay) | Area-weighted interpolation of a panel onto non-nested units. |
| [overlay_weights](#geo4bolivia.aggregate.overlay_weights) | Area of every source-target intersection, in an equal-area projection. |

### aggregate { #geo4bolivia.aggregate.aggregate }

```python
aggregate.aggregate(
    df,
    to,
    dataset=None,
    rules=None,
    variables=None,
    weights=None,
    ref=None,
)
```

Aggregate a panel up to a coarser, nested scale (or to several at once).

#### Parameters {.doc-section .doc-section-parameters}

| Name      | Type                 | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    | Default    |
|-----------|----------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------|
| df        | pd.DataFrame         | Panel with ``geo_id``, optionally ``year``, and variable columns                                                                                                                                                                                               | _required_ |
| to        |                      | Target scale id, or a list of them (e.g. ``["department", "country"]``), each containing the source scale (see ``geo.ancestors``)                                                                                                                              | _required_ |
| dataset   | str \| None          | Take each variable's ``geo:aggregation`` rule from this dataset's metadata                                                                                                                                                                                     | `None`     |
| rules     | dict \| None         | Explicit rules that override the metadata, e.g. ``{"y": "weighted_mean:pop"}``                                                                                                                                                                                 | `None`     |
| variables |                      | Variables to aggregate (default: every numeric column except ids and year, leaving out, with a warning, those without a rule)                                                                                                                                  | `None`     |
| weights   | pd.DataFrame \| None | Extra columns to join on ``geo_id`` (and ``year`` if present) for ``weighted_mean`` rules whose weight is not already in ``df``. When the dataset's metadata names where a weight lives (``geo:weights``, e.g. census population), it is loaded automatically. | `None`     |

#### Returns {.doc-section .doc-section-returns}

| Name   | Type             | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |
|--------|------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|        | pandas.DataFrame | One row per target unit (and year) with ``n_units``, the number of source units with data for at least one of the variables, and ``coverage``, their share of all the source units in the target (1.0 when every unit has data). With a list of targets, the results are stacked with a ``scale`` column. |

### overlay { #geo4bolivia.aggregate.overlay }

```python
aggregate.overlay(
    df,
    target,
    extensive=(),
    intensive=(),
    source=None,
    id_col='geo_id',
)
```

Area-weighted interpolation of a panel onto non-nested units.

#### Parameters {.doc-section .doc-section-parameters}

| Name      | Type         | Description                                                                                                       | Default    |
|-----------|--------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------|
| df        | pd.DataFrame | Panel with ``geo_id``, optionally ``year``, and the variables                                                     | _required_ |
| target    |              | A scale id or a GeoDataFrame with ``id_col`` and polygons (e.g. a grid)                                           | _required_ |
| extensive |              | Counts/totals (population, GDP): split by the share of each source unit's area falling in the target, then summed | `()`       |
| intensive |              | Rates/averages (GDP per capita, HDI): area-weighted mean over the parts of source units inside the target         | `()`       |
| source    |              | The source scale or GeoDataFrame (default: inferred from ``geo_id``)                                              | `None`     |

#### Notes {.doc-section .doc-section-notes}

Assumes values are spread uniformly within each source unit. Returns one row
per target unit (and year), with ``geo_id`` holding the target ids.

### overlay_weights { #geo4bolivia.aggregate.overlay_weights }

```python
aggregate.overlay_weights(source, target, id_col='geo_id')
```

Area of every source-target intersection, in an equal-area projection.

#### Parameters {.doc-section .doc-section-parameters}

| Name   | Type   | Description                                              | Default    |
|--------|--------|----------------------------------------------------------|------------|
| source |        | Scale ids, or GeoDataFrames with ``id_col`` and polygons | _required_ |
| target |        | Scale ids, or GeoDataFrames with ``id_col`` and polygons | _required_ |
| id_col | str    | The id column of GeoDataFrame inputs                     | `'geo_id'` |

#### Returns {.doc-section .doc-section-returns}

| Name   | Type             | Description                                                                                                                                  |
|--------|------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|        | pandas.DataFrame | Columns ``geo_id_from``, ``geo_id_to``, ``area_km2``, ``share_of_from`` (the part of the source unit inside the target) and ``share_of_to``. |

## Example {.doc-section .doc-section-example}

```python
!pip install -q geo4bolivia
import geo4bolivia as geo
import plotly.express as px

df = geo.load("sdg04", variables="index_sdg4")
up = geo.aggregate(df, to=["department", "country"], dataset="sdg04")
up = geo.add_names(up[up["scale"] == "department"])
px.bar(up, x="name", y="index_sdg4", color="year", barmode="group",
       labels=geo.labels(up), title="Education index by department (weighted)")
```

